  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/.... \nThe subtropical high will remain entrenched across the region \nthrough at least the end of the week, with 500 mb height fields \nremaining steady between 595-597dam. Therefore, temperatures will \ncontinue to run a good 4-7 degrees above normal as afternoon highs \nacross the lower deserts will range between 108-113 degrees. There \nwill also be little overnight relief as an increase in low- level \nmoisture will cause overnight lows to only bottom out between the \nmid 80s to low 90s with the low 90s expected in the Phoenix area. \nTherefore, widespread Major HeatRisk will continue with Extreme Heat \nWarnings currently in effect through Friday evening. Extension of \nthis warning may be warranted through next weekend as a weakening \nand slight re-positioning of the high further away may not come \nuntil Sunday-Monday next week.\n\nThe latest guidance does show the high eventually moving northeast \ninto the Four Corners region by the end of the week into next \nweekend, putting the region under a more favorable moisture \nadvection pattern as the east to southeast flow increases. \nTherefore, an increase in monsoon activity is more likely during \nthis timeframe, mostly across the higher terrain areas. Although, it \nwill certainly be possible for convective activity to impact the \nsouth-central AZ lower deserts at some point as well, it is still \ntoo far out to know what days will be most active as it will be \ndependent on mesoscale processes such as outflow evolutions and/or \nany easterly waves, which are difficult for models to resolve \nseveral days out. As of right now, NBM PoPs across the south-central \nAZ lower deserts starting next Thursday range between 10-30%.",
